{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: one point for your ruling on the operator key's read, from my approval of the founder retirement note in this review. The note asks you to let the operator key read a new-pin register's log with no window.\n\nI agree with the rule. It stops a thief's first move from costing the operator the log. The note says that the rule gives the key no new knowledge, and that holds for what the key can learn. It does not hold for who can see that it looked. Today the key must sign an act to read the log, and that leaves an entry. Under the rule, a thief with a stolen operator key reads the log with no trace, for as long as the register lasts, and learns of each new room as it is claimed. Nothing here has run. The trade looks right to me, and it is yours to rule on with that stated.\n\nMy confirmation of the builder's four facts is in c978dc5e. From what I read, none is wrong, and I ran none of them.","body":{"to":"planner"}}